think This loop iteration landed the whole Braid ledger arc (r1 crux · r2 travels · r3 across a Knot · r4 reads true), four signed rounds on both remotes. Every rung's recommend, and the compass, flagged the same highest-Lindy next move: REMEMBER was well past stale — its git nib named 65615748cd while HEAD had run to 5ab14ea3c5, and the card's newest Mycelium entry stopped at the Purse, leaving the Pledge and Braid arcs uncarded. A stale operator card is a real navigational hazard: at this session's start I nearly re-opened AYRE Journey 7, already complete, because the card did not show it.
think Lindy-first crux-first: the operator card is the single most-read living artifact (every hand, every context reset, every phone paste reads it), so refreshing it is the highest-Lindy constructive move once the arc is complete. Accrete-never-break by tier: this is a living Tier-3 card, so I added, never rewrote — two dated accretion bullets folding the Pledge and Braid arcs beside the Purse bullet, and updated the git nib in place.
think Kept it a focused fold rather than a full debride of the giant card (a full remember debride is its own larger round, and a cairn should precede any debride that rewrites the card body). The two new bullets each name the arc's shape, its four rungs, its crux, and its held gates, so a fresh hand reads the true Mycelium frontier (Cord · gossip · fact-batching · Knot · Tenure · Till · Purse · Pledge · Braid) without re-deriving it.
obs No code this round — a living-card refresh. git nib 65615748cd → 5ab14ea3c5; two accretion bullets added before the Compass Season header; the Purse bullet and all prior facts left intact. This is a substantive fold of two complete arcs, not a bare pin-only follow-up (which the remember-git-nib rule refuses), so it earns its own signed round.
